<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bozrah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Busayra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bozrah was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom of Edom, identified with the site of Busayra in southern Jordan, south of Tafilah. Excavations by Crystal-M. Bennett in the 1970s uncovered a fortified town with a large palace or temple complex, mainly of the 8th to 6th centuries BCE, when Edom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> mentions it in prophecies against Edom; it is distinct from Bosra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bozrah.jpg" alt="Bozrah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Uri~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazra.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
